Working with UUU
----------------

The Universal Update Utility (UUU) by NXP is software to execute on the host for
loading and running the bootloader on the board through SDP (Serial Download
Protocol). For detailed information visit https://github.com/nxp-imx/mfgtools or
download the `Official UUU-tool documentation
<https://community.nxp.com/pwmxy87654/attachments/pwmxy87654/imx-processors/140261/1/UUU.pdf>`_.

Host preparations for UUU Usage
...............................

A recent version of UUU is often available as an official package in your Linux
distribution. Obtain it using the package manager of your host distribution.

Alternatively, if no recent version of UUU is available in your host
distribution, install UUU from source:

*  Follow the instructions from https://github.com/nxp-imx/mfgtools#linux.

*  Add it to ``PATH``:

   .. code-block:: sh

      export PATH=~/mfgtools/uuu/:"$PATH"

   This BASH command adds ``uuu`` only temporarily to ``PATH``. To add it
   permanently, the line to ``~/.bashrc``.

*  Set udev rules (documented in ``uuu -udev``):

   .. code-block:: console

      host:~$ sudo sh -c "uuu -udev > /etc/udev/rules.d/70-uuu.rules"
      host:~$ sudo udevadm control --reload

Get Images
..........

Download imx-boot from our server or get it from your Yocto build directory at
:substitution-code:`build/deploy-|yocto-distro|/images/|yocto-machinename|/`.
For flashing a wic image to e.MMC, you will also need
:substitution-code:`|yocto-imagename|-|yocto-machinename|.rootfs.wic`.

Prepare Target
..............

Set the |ref-bootswitch| to **USB Serial Download**. Also, connect USB port
|ref-usb-otg| to your host.

Starting bootloader via UUU-Tool
................................

Execute and power up the board:

.. code-block:: console

   host:~$ uuu -b spl imx-boot

You can see the bootlog on the console via |ref-debugusbconnector|, as usual.

.. note::
   The default boot command when booting with UUU is set to ``fastboot``. If you
   want to change this, please adjust the environment variable ``bootcmd_mfg``
   in U-Boot prompt with ``env set bootcmd_mfg ...``. Please note, when booting
   with UUU the default environment is loaded. ``saveenv`` and ``env save`` have
   no effect. If you want to change the boot command permanently for uuu-boot,
   you need to change this in U-Boot source code.

.. uuu-flash-emmc-marker

Flashing U-Boot Image to e.MMC via UUU
......................................

.. warning::

   UUU flashes U-Boot into e.MMC hardware boot partitions, and it sets the
   ``BOOT_PARTITION_ENABLE`` in the e.MMC! This is a problem since we want the
   bootloader to reside in the e.MMC user area. Flashing next U-Boot version WIC
   image and not disabling ``BOOT_PARTITION_ENABLE`` bit will result in devices
   always loading U-Boot from e.MMC boot partitions. To fix this in U-Boot:

   .. code-block:: console
      :substitutions:

      u-boot=> mmc partconf |u-boot-emmc-devno| 0 0 0
      u-boot=> mmc partconf |u-boot-emmc-devno|
      EXT_CSD[179], PARTITION_CONFIG:
      BOOT_ACK: 0x0
      BOOT_PARTITION_ENABLE: 0x0
      PARTITION_ACCESS: 0x0

   Alternatively, check :ref:`Disable booting from e.MMC boot partitions
   <emmc-disable-boot-part>` from Linux.

   This way, the bootloader is still being flashed to e.MMC boot partitions, but
   it is not used.

   When using **partup** and ``.partup`` packages for e.MMC flashing, this is
   done by default, which makes partup again a superior flash option.

Execute the following command and then power up or reset the board:

.. code-block:: console

   host:~$ uuu -b emmc imx-boot

Flashing WIC Images to e.MMC via UUU
....................................

Execute the following command and then power up or reset the board:

.. code-block:: console
   :substitutions:

   host:~$ uuu -b emmc_all imx-boot |yocto-imagename|-|yocto-machinename|.rootfs.wic

Flashing partup Packages to e.MMC via UUU
.........................................

Flashing partup packages via UUU to the target e.MMC is done by writing to the
exposed UMS (USB mass storage) device of the board. For this to work, the U-Boot
image being loaded via SDP needs a modified ``bootcmd_mfg``, so it does not boot
into fastboot mode.

In the U-Boot source code, modify the board's environment file:

.. code-block::
   :substitutions:

   bootcmd_mfg=ums 0 mmc |u-boot-emmc-devno|

With the modified binary, run imx-boot on the board:

.. code-block:: console

   host:~$ uuu -b spl imx-boot

This will automatically start ``ums`` on the target and expose the e.MMC to the
host. The U-Boot may output the following:

.. code-block::
   :substitutions:

   Run bootcmd_mfg: ums 0 mmc |u-boot-emmc-devno|
   Hit any key to stop autoboot:  0
   UMS: LUN 0, dev mmc |u-boot-emmc-devno|, hwpart 0, sector 0x0, count 0x3a3e000

On the host side, the device should appear as a UMS device:

.. code-block:: console

   host:~$ lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,LABEL,MODEL
   NAME          SIZE LABEL  MODEL
   ...
   sdd          29.1G        UMS disk 0

This device, ``/dev/sdd`` in this example, behaves exactly like an SD card. The
e.MMC user area is fully available and ready for flashing with partup. Replace
``/dev/sdd`` with the actual device found on your host.

.. code-block::
   :substitutions:

   host:~$ partup install |yocto-imagename|-|yocto-machinename|.rootfs.partup /dev/sdd

.. note::
   e.MMC-specific features, like `e.MMC boot partitions
   <https://partup.readthedocs.io/en/latest/layout-config-reference.html#emmc-boot-partitions>`_,
   `hwreset and bootbus configuration
   <https://partup.readthedocs.io/en/latest/layout-config-reference.html#mmc-specific-controls>`_,
   are not available this way. This is a fundamental limitation of this approach
   using UUU and UMS devices. If e.MMC-specific features are needed, it is
   advised to boot a minimal Linux system on the target and run partup there.

.. uuu-flash-sdcard-marker

Flashing U-Boot to SD Card via UUU
..................................

Execute the following command and then power up or reset the board:

.. code-block:: console

   host:~$ uuu -b sd imx-boot

Flashing WIC Images to SD Card via UUU
......................................

Execute the following command and then power up or reset the board:

.. code-block:: console
   :substitutions:

   host:~$ uuu -b sd_all imx-boot |yocto-imagename|-|yocto-machinename|.rootfs.wic

.. uuu-flash-spinor-marker

Flashing SPI NOR Flash via UUU
..............................

Execute the following command and then power up or reset the board:

.. code-block:: console
   :substitutions:

   host:~$ uuu -b qspi imx-boot-|yocto-machinename|-fspi.bin-flash_evk_flexspi

This will update the U-Boot on SPI NOR Flash but not the environment. You may
need to erase the old environment so the default environment of the new U-Boot
gets loaded:

.. code-block:: console

   u-boot=> env erase
   u-boot=> reset
